 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Versions",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Two output arrangements are offered, both in SO-8. The separated-output version brings the source and sink transistors out to GON and GOFF, so a different gate resistor can be fitted for turn-on and for turn-off. The single-output version combines them on GOUT and uses pin 7 for an active Miller clamp, aimed at stopping false turn-on spikes during fast switching in half bridges."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gate supply and UVLO",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "VH, measured from GNDISO, can supply a unipolar drive (gate between GNDISO and VH) or a bipolar one, where GNDISO is taken below the switch emitter or source by a separate negative supply. VH carries undervoltage lockout with fixed hysteresis: below VHoff the output goes to safe state, and it resumes following the inputs once VH climbs back to VHon."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply bypassing",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Both VDD and VH need local decoupling with low-ESR ceramic SMT parts: a 100 nF capacitor right at each supply pin pair (VDD to GND, VH to GNDISO), plus a 1 µF to 10 µF capacitor near it for the pulsed gate current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Safe state",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "In safe state GOFF is on, GON is high impedance and, on the STGAP2SC, CLAMP is on. The device holds this state while the isolated side powers up (VH below VHon) and through power-down (VH below VHoff), whatever the inputs are. If VH is too low to drive the internal sink transistor, or is floating, a passive structure still clamps GOFF to no more than SafeClp (2.3 V max at 0.2 A). With VDD missing the output is also in safe state until VDD returns to its operating range. With both sides powered the output follows the inputs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Control inputs",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "IN+ is active high and IN- active low; the output is on only with IN+ high and IN- low. Wiring one controller signal to IN+ of one driver and IN- of the other gives hardware interlock against shoot-through in a half bridge. A deglitch filter rejects input pulses shorter than tdeglitch (20 ns typ, 40 ns max). The inputs accept 3.3 V and 5 V CMOS/TTL levels with hysteresis and have internal pull-downs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Miller clamp",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "On the STGAP2SC the CLAMP pin watches the switch gate while it is off. When the gate is below VCLAMPth (2 V typ) the clamp transistor shorts gate to GNDISO, so current injected through the gate-drain capacitance when the other switch of the leg turns on cannot lift the gate and turn the switch on."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Watchdog",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The isolated side checks that it is still in contact with the logic side. If the link is lost, for instance because VDD is unpowered, the output goes to safe state until the link is restored."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal shutdown",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "At a junction temperature of TSD (170 °C min) the device enters safe state; it resumes when the junction has cooled below TSD - Thys."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Standby",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Holding IN+ and IN- both high for longer than tSTBY puts the device into standby: supply currents fall to IQDDSBY and IQHSBY and the output is actively held low in safe state. The inputs may move while in standby. To wake it, the inputs are first set to any other combination for longer than tstbyfilt, then returned to both-high for a time between tWUP and tSTBY. tawake after the inputs leave the both-high code, the output comes back on and follows them."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Timing definitions",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Propagation delay tDon runs from the 50 % point of the input edge to the 10 % point of the output rise; tDoff runs from the 50 % point of the input edge to the 90 % point of the output fall. Rise time tr spans 10 % to 90 % of the output, fall time tf 90 % to 10 %. Either input can switch the output."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "The 100 nF capacitors sit at the pins, VDD to GND and VH to GNDISO, with the 1 µF to 10 µF capacitors beside them; all are low-ESR, low-ESL ceramics. Small filter capacitors on IN+ and IN- help in fast or noisy designs. The power transistors sit close to the driver so the gate loop has little area and inductance. No trace or copper runs under the package, which would reduce the isolation. On multilayer boards VH and GNDISO reach inner planes through several vias next to the pins, which also carry heat away. The example STGAP2SC half-bridge board carries, per driver, RIN and CIN on the inputs, CVDD1, CVH1 and CVH2, and RON, ROFF, DOFF and CG at the gate, with DBOOT and RBOOT on the board as well."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Isolation",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Isolation is rated to VDE 0884-11 (2720 Vpeak type test, 3200 Vpeak production test, 4800 Vpeak transient and surge) and recognised under UL 1577 (2828 Vrms for 1 minute). Clearance and creepage between the input and output terminals are each 4 mm, with CTI of at least 400 V, material group II."
  }
 ],
